<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Idel-Ural State</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Kazan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tatar-Bashkir national state proclaimed on the Volga in 1918 and suppressed by the Bolsheviks within weeks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>, Muslim congresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and Ufa debated autonomy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a>. In late 1917 the National Assembly of Muslims of Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> resolved to create the Idel-Ural State, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuvash-people">Chuvash</a>, with Kazan as capital, and its proclamation was planned for March 1918.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The project was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> politicians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadri-maqsudi">Sadri Maqsudi</a> and Galimjan Sharaf, envisaging a democratic federal state with Muslim institutions and minority rights. It had a militia based in the Tatar quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, across the Bulaq canal, known as the 'Zabulachnaya Republic', but almost no administration beyond the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bolshevik forces arrested its leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in February 1918 and dispersed the defenders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> quarter in March, ending the state. The Soviets later created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar ASSR</a> in 1920. The Idel-Ural idea survived among Tatar émigrés.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/idel-ural-state.jpg" alt="Idel-Ural State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Idel-Ural_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kazan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sadri Maqsudi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tatar, Bashkir</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Idel-Ural Republic</dd></dl></aside></div>

